--TEST--
`Core\Script::finish()` ends the script and every `finally` between the call and the root runs on the way — `rule:observability/three-endings-fire-the-exit-queue` and `rule:errors/propagation`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The whole point of the ending, and the one thing that makes it not `exit`
// under another name: the marker is raised on the *throw* path, which is the
// path a `finally` body lives on, so every enclosing region runs its own copy
// on the way out. A design that reached the root by a fifth status would print
// only `start` and the hook line.
Core\Script::onExit(fn (Core\Script\ExitReport $report): void => {
    echo "the queue still drains\n";
});

class Deeper {
    public static function run(): void {
        try {
            Core\Script::finish();
        } finally {
            // A `finally` one frame *below* the one that named the ending. The
            // re-propagate carries the marker frame by frame, so this runs
            // before its caller's does rather than going with the frame.
            echo "callee finally\n";
        }
    }
}

echo "start\n";
try {
    try {
        Deeper::run();
    } finally {
        echo "inner finally\n";
    }
} finally {
    echo "outer finally\n";
}
// Nothing after the call runs: the block the call sits in is sealed by the
// raise, so this is dead in every frame the marker passed through.
echo "this line never runs\n";
--EXPECT--
start
callee finally
inner finally
outer finally
the queue still drains
